{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Nobody depends on a prototype and the artifact is meant to be deleted, so exploration speed dominates and reading the code adds nothing. The licence is conditional: label it as disposable, and never let a prototype acquire real users or production credentials."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo has to work in front of an audience, and PoCs have a strong habit of quietly becoming the real product, so verify behavior deliberately before you present. Review selectively rather than line by line; reading every line of code you intend to throw away is wasted effort."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Early users are forgiving and the product will be substantially rewritten as you learn, so line-by-line review would cost more than it saves. Stay at 3 but drop to 2 for anything touching authentication, payments, personal data, or actions a user cannot easily undo."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Once paying customers depend on the code and a team must maintain it, unread code becomes a liability: nobody can debug, extend, or vouch for it under pressure. AI can write it, but a person reviews every change and owns it, with 3 reserved for genuinely low-risk changes covered by tests."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "When failure can injure people, break the law, or breach a regulator's requirements, a human must be the author of record rather than the reviewer of a machine's output. Use AI to draft, explain, and test, but have an engineer write or rewrite every line that ships; specific certified artifacts may need 0."
    }
  },
  "summary": "The appropriate level falls as the cost of being wrong rises, so treat these as ceilings rather than targets: 4 for disposable exploration, 3 while code is still cheap to discard, 2 once customers depend on it, and 1 where safety or law is at stake. Pick the level per change by blast radius rather than per project by stage, since a throwaway script inside a mature system can take 4 and a prototype holding real customer data cannot."
}

